 ### Dynamic Octree Generation
-A technique used in Mega-NeRF to manage octree size by bounding it to available GPU memory and pruning nodes that do not contribute to rendering [S2]. It differs from static residency updates by actively growing and shrinking the tree based on rendering visibility rather than just cache residency [S2].
-*   **Definition**: Track the number of pixels visible from each node during rendering traversal. Subdivide the top $k$ nodes with the most visible pixels. Prune entries that have not recently contributed to reclaim space when capacity is hit [S2].
-*   **Parameters**: Maximum tree size: 20M elements. Top $k$ nodes to subdivide: 16,384 [S2].
-*   **Number**: Maximum tree depth observed in practice: roughly 12 [S2].
-*   **When to choose**: Use when memory is constrained and the scene has varying levels of detail that can be dynamically adjusted based on camera view, rather than when managing a fixed set of pre-computed volume bricks [S2]. See also [Decoupling Resolution Levels from Spatial Subdivision](5250-decoupling-resolution-levels-from-spatial-subdivision.md).
+A technique used in Mega-NeRF to manage octree size by bounding it to available GPU memory and pruning nodes that do not contribute to rendering [S2]. It differs from static residency updates by actively growing and shrinking the tree based on rendering visibility rather than just cache residency [S2]. The method tracks the number of pixels visible from each node during rendering traversal, subdivides the top $k$ nodes with the most visible pixels, and prunes entries that have not recently contributed to reclaim space when capacity is hit [S2]. The maximum tree size is bounded to 20M elements, the top $k$ nodes to subdivide is 16,384, and the maximum tree depth observed in practice is roughly 12 [S2]. It should be chosen when memory is constrained and the scene has varying levels of detail that can be dynamically adjusted based on camera view, rather than when managing a fixed set of pre-computed volume bricks [S2]. See also [Decoupling Resolution Levels from Spatial Subdivision](5250-decoupling-resolution-levels-from-spatial-subdivision.md).
 
